posts.plaintext:
The authors of Raft Consensus Algorithm [https://raft.github.io] made
understandability their primary objective. The paper
[https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf] itself is superbly structured and. 

This is an attempt to TDD. Word-for-word following the paper 

Raft
The algorithm itself is described in terms on a replicated state machine. The
objective is to ensure that all participating servers agrees on the same state.
This is usually done by a replication log. 

A replication log is just a list of change, [add(a), delete(b), ...]. If we can
have all the machines concluded on the same list of change. We can then replay
the log and produce a consistent state across all machines

Therefore, Raft decomposed distributed consensus into 3 problems. 

 1. Leader Election 
    Only 1 node in a group claim leadership at a time. It is responsible to
    replicate logs to other nodes. The focus here is to make sure every nodes
    agree on the same leader.
 2. Log Replication 
    Leader node, that every node agrees on, accepts client requests make ensure
    that it is replicated to other nodes.
 3. Safety
    Additional Rules imposed to (1) (2), glued them together to make sure the
    algorithm is correct 

Paper Outline
Skimming the paper, to see the general idea and visual aid. There are some

 * 5.1 Raft Basics - explains the nodes, their states, and overall protocol 
 * Figure 2 - summarize all states and protocols. Not needed now. Good to know
   that it is here

Leader Election
We will just dive head first into the protocol 

Server statesThen we will follow 5.2 Leader election. This is word-for-word

> When servers start up, they begin as followers.
Simply assign an initial role to server as Follower 

class Server {
  val role = Follower
}

class RaftTest {
  val server = Server()

  @Nested inner class LeaderElection {
    @Test fun `When servers start up, they begin as followers`() {
      assertEquals(Follower, server.role)
    }
  }
}

> A server remains in follower state as long as it receives valid RPCs from a
leader or candidate.
It is yet to be defined what a valid RPC is. So at this point it could be any
RPC. Although a test we create won't fail, let's put in something so we don't
forget. Also a good time to introduce receives as dictated. 

class RPC {}

class Server {
  val role = Follower

  fun receives(rpc: RPC) {}
}

@Test fun `When server receives an RPC, remains a follower`() {
  server.receives(RPC())
  assertEquals(Follower, server.role)
}

> Leaders send periodic heartbeats (AppendEntries RPCs that carry no log entries)
to all followers in order to maintain their authority
This seems way too complex to implement now as we haven't even talk about how a
server gets to be a leader. Let's just create an @Ignore test to remind us of
the case. 

@Ignore @Test fun `Leaders send periodic AppendEntriesRPCs with no logEntries) to all followers to maintain their authority`() {
}

> If a follower receives no communication over a period of time called the 
election timeout, then it assumes there is no viable leader and begins an
election to choose a new leader. To begin an election, a follower increments its
current term and transitions to candidate state.
Again, electionTimeout seems a bit too complex for now. It is clear what it
means by begin an election though. 

class Server {
  var role = Follower
  var term = 0

  fun beginElection() {
    role = Candidate
    term++
  }
}

    @Ignore @Test fun `If a follower receives no communication over electionTimeout, begin a new election`() {
    }

    @Test fun `Begin an election, a follower increments its current term and becomes candidate`() {
      with (server) {
        beginElection()
        assertEquals(Candidate, role)
        assertEquals(1, term)
      }
    }

> It then votes for itself and issues RequestVote RPCs in parallel to each of the
other servers in the cluster.
There's a lot in this simple sentence. It can be broken down into

 * The server votes for itself means changing votedFor value to to the server
   id. 
 * Implicitly this means initial value for votedFor is null/empty. Confirmed
   that with the state in figure 2
 * RequestVoteRPC being sent carries some information. It seems only term and 
   candidateId is important for now
 * There needs to be something that facilities sending messages to all other
   servers in parallel. Let's try to stay in domain of one server and
   encapsulate this complexity in rpcClient that is mocked for now. 

To recap. We are only asserting that votedFor is set and RPC are being issued
correctly to the rpcClient. Adds some more @Ignored test to implement the whole 
RPCClient later. 

For mocking duty, let's bring in MockK [https://github.com/mockk/mockk] to do
the job.

interface RPC 
// Define our first RPC with parameter enough to be meaningful in asserts
data class RequestVoteRPC(val term: Int, val candidateId: Int) : RPC

  fun beginElection() {
    ...
    votedFor = id
    rpcClient.sendToAll(RequestVoteRPC(term, id))
  }

    @Test fun `At election, candidate votes for itself and issue RequestVoteRPC in parallel to all servers`() {
      with (server) {
        beginElection()
        assertEquals(mainServerId, votedFor)
        verify { rpcClient.sendToAll(RequestVoteRPC(server.term, mainServerId)) }
      }
    }

    @Test fun `When servers start up, votedFor is null`() {
      assertNull(server.votedFor)
    }

    @Ignore @Test fun `Implements RPCClient sendToAll() to deliver messages to client in parallel`() {

    }



> A candidate continues in this state until one of three things happens: (a) it
wins the election, (b) another server establishes itself as leader, or (c) a
period of time goes by with no winner.
There are three outcomes after the server becomes a candidate. Each with
appropriate response defined 

Wins an Election
> A candidate wins an election if it receives votes from a majority of the servers
in the full cluster for the same term.
Seems like we need to track how many votes we have so far, along with how many
servers actually is in the cluster. The actual response is outlined in figure 2.
It has two fields (voteGranted, term). 

"Win the election" is clarified a few lines below that "Once a candidate wins an
election, it becomes leader". 

Easiest thing we could do to pass is to make it pass on a RequestVoteRPC
response. Then add a test later to see that it needs 3 granted votes

data class RequestVoteRPCResponse(val term: Int, val voteGranted: Boolean): RPC

//V1: Incorrect but pass the test 
  fun receives(rpc: RPC) {
    when(rpc) {
      is RequestVoteRPCResponse -> {
        role = Leader
      }
    }
  }

    @Test fun `A candidate becomes leader if it receives votes from a majority of the servers in same term`() {
      with(server) {
        beginElection()
        repeat(3) { receives(RequestVoteRPCResponse(term, true)) }
        assertEquals(Leader, server.role)
      }
    }

Then we implement the other constraints that we can deduce directly from the
requirements

 1. We have to keep track of the votes per server, not only the current server
 2. Only `voteGranted = true` and same term count
 3. The votes are cleared when we begin election

Which translate into these tests (so many copy and paste...)

    @Nested inner class CandidateReceivedVotes {
      @Test
      fun `A candidate becomes leader if it receives votes from a majority of the servers in same term`() {
        with(server) {
          beginElection()
          for (i in 2..3) {
            receives(RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, term, true))
          }
          assertEquals(Leader, server.role)
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `A candidate with rejected votes does not count`() {
        with(server) {
          beginElection()
          for (i in 2..3) {
            receives(RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, term, false))
          }
          assertEquals(Candidate, server.role)
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `A candidate with votes from different terms does not count`() {
        with(server) {
          beginElection()
          for (i in 2..3) {
            receives(RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, term + 1, true))
          }
          assertEquals(Candidate, server.role)
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `A repeated votes from same server does not count`() {
        val server = Server(mainServerId, rpcClient, 5)
        with(server) {
          beginElection()
          repeat(5) { receives(RequestVoteRPCResponse(2, term, true)) }
          assertEquals(Candidate, server.role)
        }
      }
    }


Spent some time to clean up

  fun beginElection() {
    ...
    hasVotesFrom = mutableSetOf()
    ...
  }

  fun receives(rpc: RPC) {
    when(rpc) {
      is RequestVoteRPCResponse -> {
        if (rpc.voteGranted && rpc.term == term) {
          hasVotesFrom.add(rpc.from)
        }

        if (hasVotesFrom.size + 1 > numServers / 2) {
          role = Leader
        }
      }
    }
  }

    @Nested inner class CandidateReceivedVotes {
      @Test
      fun `A candidate becomes leader if it receives votes from a majority of the servers in same term and the vote is cleared`() {
        assertRoleAfterVotes(Leader, { i -> RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, server.term, true) })
      }

      @Test
      fun `A candidate with rejected votes does not count`() {
        assertRoleAfterVotes(Candidate, { i -> RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, server.term, false) })
      }

      @Test
      fun `A candidate with votes from different terms does not count`() {
        assertRoleAfterVotes(Candidate, { i -> RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, server.term + 1, true) })
      }

      @Test
      fun `A repeated votes from same server does not count`() {
        val server = Server(mainServerId, rpcClient, 5)
        assertRoleAfterVotes(Candidate, { i -> RequestVoteRPCResponse(2, server.term, true) })
      }

      @Test
      fun `Votes are clear when we begin election again`() {
        assertRoleAfterVotes(Leader, { i -> RequestVoteRPCResponse(i, server.term, true) })
        server.beginElection()
        assertEquals(mainServerId, server.votedFor)
        assertEquals(setOf<Int>(), server.hasVotesFrom)
      }


      private fun assertRoleAfterVotes(expectedRole: Role, rpc: (repeatIndex:Int) -> RequestVoteRPCResponse ) {
        with(server) {
          beginElection()
          for (i in 2..3) { receives(rpc(i)) }
          assertEquals(expectedRole, server.role)
        }
      }
    }

> Each server will vote for at most one candidate in a given term, on a
first-come-first-served basis.
Implement above spec on a receiving side of RequestVoteRPC, which is not quite
complete without auxiliary info found again in Figure 2 that we should reject
calls when term is less than current

> Reply false if term < currentTerm
  fun receives(rpc: RPC) {
    when(rpc) {
      ...

      is RequestVoteRPC -> {
        if( (votedFor == null || votedFor == rpc.candidateId) && rpc.term >= term) {
          rpcClient.send(id, rpc.candidateId, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, rpc.term, true))
          votedFor = rpc.candidateId
        } else {
          rpcClient.send(id, rpc.candidateId, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, rpc.term, false))
        }
      }
    }
  }

  @Nested inner class ReceivedRequestVoteRPC {
      val requestedFrom = 2

      @Test
      fun `Voted for the candidate if hasn't been voted for any`() {
        with(server) {
          receives(RequestVoteRPC(term, requestedFrom))
          verify { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, term, true)) }
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `After voted for candidate, we shouldn't vote for others in the same term`() {
        with(server) {
          receives(RequestVoteRPC(term, requestedFrom))
          receives(RequestVoteRPC(term, requestedFrom + 1))

          verify { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, term, true)) }
          verify(exactly = 0) { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom + 1, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, term, true)) }
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `After voted for candidate, can vote again for same server`() {
        with(server) {
          receives(RequestVoteRPC(term, requestedFrom))
          receives(RequestVoteRPC(term, requestedFrom))

          verify(exactly = 2) { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, term, true)) }
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `Reply false if term is less than current term`() {
        with(server) {
          receives(RequestVoteRPC(term - 1, requestedFrom))

          verify { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, term - 1, false)) }
        }
      }
    }

> Once a candidate wins an election, it becomes leader. It then sends heartbeat
messages to all of the other servers to establish its authority and prevent new
elections.
We already have @Ignored test for this case

Another Server Established as Leader
> A candidate may receive an AppendEntries RPC from another server claiming to be
leader. If the leader’s term (included in its RPC) is at least as large as the
candidate’s current term, then the candidate recognizes the leader as legitimate
and returns to follower state.
One message to test for, two conditions. 

      is AppendEntriesRPC -> {
        if(rpc.term >= term) {
          rpcClient.send(id, rpc.leaderId, AppendEntriesRPCResponse(id, term, true))
          role = Follower
        } else {
          rpcClient.send(id, rpc.leaderId, AppendEntriesRPCResponse(id, term, false))
        }
      }

    @Nested inner class CandidateGoBackToFollower {
      val requestedFrom = 2

      @Test
      fun `when received AppendEntriesRPC call and terms as large as current, go back to Follower`() {
        with(server) {
          makeCandidate()
          receives(AppendEntriesRPC(term, requestedFrom))
          assertEquals(Follower, server.role)

          verify { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom, AppendEntriesRPCResponse(id, term, true)) }
        }
      }

      @Test
      fun `when received AppendEntriesRPC call and terms less than current, rejects and remain candidate`() {
        with(server) {
          makeCandidate()
          receives(AppendEntriesRPC(term - 1, requestedFrom))
          assertEquals(Candidate, server.role)

          verify { rpcClient.send(id, requestedFrom, AppendEntriesRPCResponse(id, term, false)) }
        }
      }

      private fun makeCandidate() {
        server.beginElection()
      }
    }

Candidate neither wins nor Lose election

> if many followers become candidates at the same time, votes could be split so
that no candidate obtains a majority. When this happens, each candidate will
time out and start a new election
> To prevent split votes in the first place, election timeouts are chosen randomly
from a fixed interval (e.g., 150–300ms)
Let's make that two @Ignored tests as it dealt with time and timeouts (again). 

    @Nested inner class CandidateStartsNewElection {
      @Ignore @Test fun `when it does not have majority vote by electionTimeout`() {}
      @Ignore @Test fun `timeout are randomly choosen`() {}
    }


Refactor
That's all the spec on paper. At this point let's step back and review the code
to see if we found something that does not read right. 

Duplicate code in RPC processing

      is RequestVoteRPC -> {
        if( (votedFor == null || votedFor == rpc.candidateId) && rpc.term >= term) {
          rpcClient.send(id, rpc.candidateId, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, rpc.term, true))
          votedFor = rpc.candidateId
        } else {
          rpcClient.send(id, rpc.candidateId, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, rpc.term, false))
        }
      }

//Becomes:

      is RequestVoteRPC -> {
        val valid = isValidRequest(rpc)
        if (valid) {
          votedFor = rpc.candidateId
        }
        rpcClient.send(id, rpc.candidateId, RequestVoteRPCResponse(id, rpc.term, valid))
      }

  private fun isValidRequest(rpc: RequestVoteRPC) =
      (votedFor == null || votedFor == rpc.candidateId) && rpc.term >= term

Terms in Request/Response

Looking back at the tests. We weren't very careful about terms Looking at Figure
2, the server should maintain currentTerm to be the latest it has seen. In both
types of RPC, the result contains term for either candidate or leader to update. 

Going back to (5.1) Raft basics, we are missing these tests. 

> Current terms are exchanged whenever servers communicate; if one server’s
current term is smaller than the other’s, then it updates its current term to
the larger value. 
> If a candidate or leader discovers that its term is out of date, it immediately
reverts to follower state. 
Also we missed that all RPC always has terms in both request/response. That is
something common for all RPCs

